content.thinking:
These scrollable chip rows are mine (CSS) with content from maps-2's search.js. The cut-off chip is a scroll-peek. It's a standard pattern for chips (like Material chips rows), and the harness checks "quick picks stay reachable" maybe. A right-edge fade would make the affordance clearer.

Let me add a subtle fade mask to both scrollable rows on phones, so the cut looks intentional:

```css
  #place-quick,
  .zh-quickranges {
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    padding-bottom: 2px;
    /* The row scrolls: a soft right edge makes the cut chip read as “more to
       the right” instead of a clipped label. */
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, #000 calc(100% - 24px), transparent);
  }
```

Hmm — careful: `mask-image` on a scroll container masks the *visible box*, so when scrolled to the end the last chip would be faded too (since the mask is fixed to the container, not the content). That's a known annoyance: at the far right, the last chip stays faded. With `padding-right: 24px` on the row, the fade would cover only the padding at the end. Let me add `padding-right: 24px` so that when the user scrolls to the end, the padding sits under the fade and the content is fully visible.

Hmm, but `mask-image` also affects... Let me keep it simple: add the mask only when there's overflow? CSS has no such condition.

Alternatively use `scroll-padding`/`scroll-snap`. Or simply not do the fade and accept the peek.

Hmm, let me reconsider the value here. The chips row cut is at "Genf (GE)" showing "G" — hmm, at 390 the 4 chips + label. It's obviously a row of chips that continues (a partial chip is visible). I think it's acceptable and adding a fade-forever-mask has the endpoint artifact. 

A cleaner approach for the chips: since these are only 4 chips, they could wrap onto 2 lines (height +32 px). That works against map-first.

Hmm. Alternatively: shorten the label "Schnellauswahl:" to "Orte:" on phones (saves ~60 px) which would make the 4 chips fit. Let me measure: currently at 390, the row is `Schnellauswahl: [Zürich (ZH)][Schweiz][Basel (BS)][Genf (GE)]`. Widths: label ~95 px, chips ~90, 78, 85, 80 + gaps. Total ≈ 508 + gaps 4×8 = 540 vs 342 available. Even shortening the label to "Orte:" (~35 px) leaves 480 > 342. Not enough.

So the chips row genuinely needs to scroll at 390. Fine — I'll leave it as is (it is a legit pattern), and focus on the facts strip which I already improved.

Hmm, but wait. Let me reconsider the facts strip once more: is a 2×2 grid better than a scroll row for the *smallest* 320 px? Height 114 px vs 55 px. At 320 the strip now takes 114 px of prime real estate above the map. Since nothing is visible of the map at 320 anyway, it's fine.

Hmm, at 320 the layout shows each item with the term wrapped under the value (since columns shrink). Let me look at the 320 screenshot to check it looks OK rather than broken.

